1
00:00:04,764 --> 00:00:10,806
[SPEAKER_00]: My concern is, eh, uh, to do jhāna is, of course, my daily practice.

2
00:00:10,866 --> 00:00:32,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You told it many times, how I should, uh, during the day, I should, uh, just... Apart from that, uh, my, uh, routine meditation in the morning, just being aware of the, uh, breath, and then the periphery body, where the breath depends on.

3
00:00:32,855 --> 00:00:32,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Mm-hmm.

4
00:00:33,982 --> 00:00:43,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the summary of... this should be the summary of my meditation, now that I'm in my practice, now that I am...

5
00:00:45,542 --> 00:00:46,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Going in two days.

6
00:00:46,643 --> 00:00:53,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I would like to have a kind of summary for that, or the guidance, maybe.

7
00:00:53,309 --> 00:00:55,470
[SPEAKER_04]: In a sense of what to do when you're back.

8
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:56,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, how to...

9
00:00:56,891 --> 00:01:06,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I saw here for two or three weeks, but like a summary for the... What do you think?

10
00:01:11,528 --> 00:01:28,733
[SPEAKER_02]: You can be aware of your body, you can be aware of your breath, your body, but why are you doing that?

11
00:01:28,893 --> 00:01:34,415
[SPEAKER_00]: My ultimate goal is to get the right feel, of course, and

12
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:46,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Just try not to do things out of desire and aversion and delusion.

13
00:01:48,356 --> 00:01:49,857
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the...

14
00:01:50,978 --> 00:01:52,178
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how are those things done?

15
00:01:59,722 --> 00:02:05,646
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you accidentally, unintentionally, do things out of greed, aversion and delusion?

16
00:02:06,697 --> 00:02:17,823
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they come up and then I shouldn't react to that greed which is coming up, which is just... Not sure that react would be the best description.

17
00:02:18,223 --> 00:02:19,264
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not about reaction.

18
00:02:19,544 --> 00:02:21,385
[SPEAKER_04]: See, reaction is, oh, I just reacted.

19
00:02:21,645 --> 00:02:22,966
[SPEAKER_04]: Somebody scares you, you react.

20
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:24,527
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't prevent that.

21
00:02:25,147 --> 00:02:25,607
[SPEAKER_04]: Act.

22
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:26,768
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, act.

23
00:02:27,168 --> 00:02:28,449
[SPEAKER_04]: Act and react is different.

24
00:02:28,489 --> 00:02:28,909
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

25
00:02:29,449 --> 00:02:30,230
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's my point.

26
00:02:30,330 --> 00:02:32,271
[SPEAKER_04]: Can those things happen by accident?

27
00:02:33,311 --> 00:02:33,732
[SPEAKER_04]: They can't.

28
00:02:34,936 --> 00:02:40,197
[SPEAKER_04]: So, greed, aversion, delusion cannot accidentally occur when I forgot or something.

29
00:02:40,217 --> 00:02:40,317
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

30
00:02:40,337 --> 00:02:42,398
[SPEAKER_04]: They're always on the level of your choice.

31
00:02:44,098 --> 00:02:45,199
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they're always chosen.

32
00:02:47,219 --> 00:02:48,239
[SPEAKER_00]: That means, eh...

33
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:52,340
[SPEAKER_00]: I choose to act out of greed?

34
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:52,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

35
00:02:53,161 --> 00:02:53,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

36
00:02:54,021 --> 00:02:54,281
[SPEAKER_04]: You do.

37
00:02:54,561 --> 00:02:55,501
[SPEAKER_04]: On some level, you do.

38
00:02:55,681 --> 00:02:56,481
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to be doing.

39
00:02:57,462 --> 00:02:58,142
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the point.

40
00:02:58,162 --> 00:02:59,062
[SPEAKER_04]: Like people...

41
00:02:59,082 --> 00:03:02,703
[SPEAKER_04]: The only way to start recognizing such choices is to start looking

42
00:03:04,168 --> 00:03:06,129
[SPEAKER_04]: Choices per se, in general.

43
00:03:07,009 --> 00:03:08,710
[SPEAKER_04]: But people think like, oh, I haven't...

44
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:13,272
[SPEAKER_04]: I acted out of greed, aversion, delusion, because I wasn't focused enough, or concentrated, or calm enough.

45
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:15,093
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you acted because you chose to act.

46
00:03:16,613 --> 00:03:26,538
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the only way to get closer to that recognition, when you choose to act out of these things, is to keep pondering on the motivation behind your actions, intentions, and choices you make throughout the day.

47
00:03:27,819 --> 00:03:34,545
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, as I said, being calm is nice, it's fine, and you can calm down, sure, but that has nothing to do with your choices.

48
00:03:35,646 --> 00:03:44,855
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you think that greed, aversion, delusion comes out of, you know, accidental non-calmness or a slip of the mind or something, they don't.

49
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:45,295
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

50
00:03:45,516 --> 00:03:48,679
[SPEAKER_04]: They came because you chose to allow them to enter.

51
00:03:48,919 --> 00:03:49,299
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

52
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,062
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's what you need to look for the problem.

53
00:03:54,498 --> 00:04:00,063
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, on top of that, if calming helps you reflect further on your choices, yes, that's fine.

54
00:04:00,964 --> 00:04:05,369
[SPEAKER_04]: But you have to abandon the view that calming will make me choose better.

55
00:04:05,649 --> 00:04:06,109
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it won't.

56
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:16,920
[SPEAKER_04]: Value of certain choices and value against certain other choices, pondering on those values and importance and danger and peril, that will help you choose better.

57
00:04:18,096 --> 00:04:24,180
[SPEAKER_04]: And that peril will not be seen by itself and it will not come as a result of, you know, watching the nostril.

58
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:29,423
[SPEAKER_04]: It will come if you make the effort to see it and think about it and remind yourself of everything that the Buddha said.

59
00:04:31,444 --> 00:04:34,746
[SPEAKER_00]: And this reminding is also, of course, an issue for me.

60
00:04:34,806 --> 00:04:38,269
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, it is... That's really where the practice is.

61
00:04:38,289 --> 00:04:50,657
[SPEAKER_04]: Like bhāvana, as in keep it in the being, maintain it, a certain context that you need to be knowing, be aware of it and clarifying it further.

62
00:04:51,397 --> 00:04:59,381
[SPEAKER_04]: So, Bhāvanā is not on the level of calming, calming, until the experience of the pleasant kind comes over, and then, I don't even think about greed, aversion and delusion.

63
00:05:00,121 --> 00:05:01,362
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that was never the problem.

64
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:04,703
[SPEAKER_04]: The problem is, could you think about greed, aversion, delusion?

65
00:05:04,963 --> 00:05:10,826
[SPEAKER_04]: And the answer is yes, because the danger has not been cultivated enough, seen enough, understood enough.

66
00:05:12,347 --> 00:05:18,189
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can do all the calming you want, the moment that calming goes away, you don't know where the problem is.

67
00:05:19,370 --> 00:05:19,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

68
00:05:19,984 --> 00:05:29,891
[SPEAKER_04]: The problem was, you are still unwittingly, yet intentionally, choosing greed, aversion, delusion on the level of body, speech or mind.

69
00:05:31,826 --> 00:05:51,721
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what it's saying to me as well, is if you are watching your intentions, your choices that you're making, and valuing the right choices and devaluing the wrong ones, then you are automatically, or by default, what starts to happen is you are aware of your body, what you are physically doing.

70
00:05:52,822 --> 00:05:53,743
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, why am I doing this?

71
00:05:53,763 --> 00:05:56,305
[SPEAKER_02]: So you are aware of your physical actions.

72
00:05:57,305 --> 00:05:58,406
[SPEAKER_02]: You're aware of what you're saying.

73
00:05:58,967 --> 00:05:59,887
[SPEAKER_02]: You're aware of your mind.

74
00:06:00,488 --> 00:06:00,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

75
00:06:02,023 --> 00:06:02,964
[SPEAKER_03]: That comes out of it.

76
00:06:03,024 --> 00:06:16,999
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to be aware of the body, hoping that you're going to know why you are doing things, because you're watching your knees, you're watching your abdomen rise and fall, and that's going to reveal why you are doing something.

77
00:06:17,019 --> 00:06:18,881
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to ask yourself, why am I doing this?

78
00:06:22,897 --> 00:06:24,399
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and that's really what mindfulness is.

79
00:06:24,479 --> 00:06:27,042
[SPEAKER_04]: If you want a method for mindfulness, it's that question.

80
00:06:27,463 --> 00:06:28,604
[SPEAKER_04]: That results in mindfulness.

81
00:06:28,624 --> 00:06:29,185
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

82
00:06:29,205 --> 00:06:33,010
[SPEAKER_04]: Not the sensation watching or belly-bobbing or whatever else people do.

83
00:06:33,030 --> 00:06:33,971
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that, that, that...

84
00:06:33,991 --> 00:06:35,993
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, you do that expecting experience.

85
00:06:36,013 --> 00:06:36,414
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

86
00:06:36,594 --> 00:06:37,916
[SPEAKER_04]: Why am I doing what I'm doing?

87
00:06:38,116 --> 00:06:40,759
[SPEAKER_04]: See, that question already puts you in a state of mindfulness.

88
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:41,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

89
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:56,570
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if you keep pushing further with such questioning, you will arrive at certain universal truths, such as, oh, whatever I'm doing is in order to avoid pain, prolong pleasure, choices I make, no matter how subtle or coarse, they always have to be rooted in in those three principles.

90
00:06:56,630 --> 00:06:59,751
[SPEAKER_04]: Greed, aversion, delusion, or non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

91
00:07:01,171 --> 00:07:03,311
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's where the problem is, and that's where the solution is.

92
00:07:11,177 --> 00:07:19,511
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue is that during the day, for example, suddenly I get, I become aware that I'm thinking about

93
00:07:21,965 --> 00:07:23,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Unpleasant thought, for example.

94
00:07:24,246 --> 00:07:26,387
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm angry about something.

95
00:07:27,287 --> 00:07:32,109
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the place where I should then practice.

96
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:34,710
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah.

97
00:07:34,730 --> 00:07:42,873
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, like, the whole point is to recognize that the practice is on the level of that liability to angry thoughts or lustful thoughts, not the actual one.

98
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:45,635
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, actual one, but that's already too late.

99
00:07:45,675 --> 00:07:46,295
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so if it's...

100
00:07:47,135 --> 00:07:50,520
[SPEAKER_04]: Actual anger or lust, yeah, you got to restrain it, not act out of it.

101
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:55,266
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, when it's not there, you shouldn't start thinking, ah, okay, I dealt with the problem.

102
00:07:55,306 --> 00:07:55,686
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

103
00:07:56,247 --> 00:08:03,476
[SPEAKER_04]: As the Buddha would often say, a monk would review his mind and ask himself, can non-arisen unwholesome things arise in this mind?

104
00:08:04,557 --> 00:08:05,278
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, oh, yes.

105
00:08:05,438 --> 00:08:10,801
[SPEAKER_04]: And now, you start cultivating the danger if such things arise.

106
00:08:11,121 --> 00:08:12,022
[SPEAKER_04]: How would they arise?

107
00:08:12,102 --> 00:08:13,323
[SPEAKER_04]: Through what choices you make?

108
00:08:13,403 --> 00:08:14,884
[SPEAKER_04]: Little thing here, little thing there.

109
00:08:14,944 --> 00:08:18,066
[SPEAKER_04]: Before you know it, mind is filled with lust and you're accepting it.

110
00:08:18,766 --> 00:08:23,650
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's always on that level of welcoming, delighting, entertaining, engaging with mentally.

111
00:08:24,250 --> 00:08:25,411
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is a matter of choice.

112
00:08:26,632 --> 00:08:29,674
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, of course, if somebody is not restrained in the body,

113
00:08:31,693 --> 00:08:34,534
[SPEAKER_04]: These subtle choices on the level of their thoughts will not be seen.

114
00:08:35,354 --> 00:08:37,095
[SPEAKER_04]: But, nevertheless, they are choices.

115
00:08:37,555 --> 00:08:45,078
[SPEAKER_04]: They are things you choose to allow, so to speak, in a sense of... have a welcoming attitude towards.

116
00:08:46,458 --> 00:08:54,982
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why you have to really, like, hammer in that sense of danger, so that you uproot the attitude of this is nice, this is good, this is friendly, this is for me.

117
00:08:56,342 --> 00:09:10,734
[SPEAKER_04]: But, you you would arrive at that, yeah, if you spend, obviously, if you keep the precepts, guide the sense doors, and then, throughout the day, whatever you're doing, ask yourself questions, endure doubt, if it's unclear,

118
00:09:11,630 --> 00:09:17,552
[SPEAKER_04]: But fundamentally recognize that whatever you choose to do on the level of body, speech, or mind, cannot happen accidentally.

119
00:09:18,072 --> 00:09:20,253
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always on the level of your choice.

120
00:09:20,653 --> 00:09:26,495
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, at the same time, see, it feels like, oh, this is so overwhelming, but at the same time, it's actually easier.

121
00:09:26,515 --> 00:09:38,399
[SPEAKER_04]: It becomes easier, because you realize, okay, so I don't need to worry about these things accidentally happening, affecting me, oppressing me, and now I have to deal with them on and on, because they just accidentally came in.

122
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:40,280
[SPEAKER_04]: That becomes impossible.

123
00:09:41,131 --> 00:09:54,461
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the clearer you become in sort of this type of bhāvana, this type of cultivation, the more confident you are, because you know, oh, no, no, it doesn't matter what circumstances are coming this way, good or bad, I'm the one who opens the door.

124
00:09:56,302 --> 00:10:03,448
[SPEAKER_04]: And the clearer you are with that, the clearer the door becomes, and the more confident and safe you feel, because you won't open the door once you know the danger.

125
00:10:04,323 --> 00:10:19,091
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you keep perpetuating the view that these disturbances of the mind happen accidentally because you were not concentrated enough or calm enough, you keep perpetuating the view that the problem comes on its... from outside on its own and you have nothing to do with it, you just have to deal with it.

126
00:10:19,651 --> 00:10:21,152
[SPEAKER_04]: And, eh... and that's not the case.

127
00:10:21,853 --> 00:10:23,774
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you will always feel undermined by it.

128
00:10:24,234 --> 00:10:32,278
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter how... how well your concentration is developed and commitment to practice, you're always going to feel undermined because you... you undermine yourself.

129
00:10:32,318 --> 00:10:33,619
[SPEAKER_04]: By putting it externally,

130
00:10:35,404 --> 00:10:49,797
[SPEAKER_04]: Whatever happens, if it's... if it's sort of unpleasant, you won't know where the problem was and you would think it was because of the circumstances and then you would try to double down in your calmness and concentration and ignoring and covering up, so that these things don't arise.

131
00:10:50,017 --> 00:10:51,198
[SPEAKER_04]: But they were never the problem.

132
00:10:51,919 --> 00:10:53,820
[SPEAKER_04]: The problem was your intentional choice.

133
00:10:54,981 --> 00:10:57,564
[SPEAKER_04]: Ignorant one, lustful one or angry one.

134
00:10:58,544 --> 00:10:58,925
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

135
00:11:03,846 --> 00:11:10,116
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if there is an anger in your mind, thoughts came up, yeah, you didn't ask for it, but you have a choice now.

136
00:11:10,617 --> 00:11:13,982
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I trying to get rid of this, acting out of anger?

137
00:11:14,643 --> 00:11:17,248
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I acting out of anger further towards what I, you know...

138
00:11:18,291 --> 00:11:26,778
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I projected as the reason of my anger, or am I able to just endure it, you know, let the mind kick and scream, but not engage with such thoughts?

139
00:11:26,798 --> 00:11:31,442
[SPEAKER_04]: Not try to, you know, get rid of them, because you can't, but you can certainly not welcome them.

140
00:11:31,462 --> 00:11:36,046
[SPEAKER_04]: So, no, you can be there in front of the door, shout and scream, but you are not welcome inside.

141
00:11:36,827 --> 00:11:42,732
[SPEAKER_04]: So, your attitude of non-welcoming, that's the line, that's what you need to develop, but not further, and not less than that.

142
00:11:45,148 --> 00:11:53,675
[SPEAKER_04]: And the only way you can develop that, and develop clarity in regards to that attitude, is keep looking for it, keep asking questions, keep reviewing.

143
00:11:54,196 --> 00:11:57,939
[SPEAKER_04]: It won't happen magically on account of a concentration experience or something.

144
00:11:57,959 --> 00:11:58,740
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just impossible.

145
00:12:04,816 --> 00:12:21,759
[SPEAKER_02]: That instruction, that the two factors for the arising of right view, being the instruction from another, the word of another, and yoniso manisikāra, which is like being able to know your intentions behind your actions.

146
00:12:22,939 --> 00:12:24,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, know the origin, exactly.

147
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:26,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowing the origin of your actions.

148
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:30,440
[SPEAKER_02]: So, having the instruction and then watching what are you doing.

149
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:31,821
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, see where it's rooted.

150
00:12:34,887 --> 00:12:40,229
[SPEAKER_04]: But what's the... what's usually the problem with that, with yoniso manasikāra?

151
00:12:43,049 --> 00:12:46,170
[SPEAKER_02]: The view, the common view.

152
00:12:47,131 --> 00:12:55,393
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so say, like you describe this to somebody, yoniso manasikāra is knowing the origin of your intentions, of your choices.

153
00:12:56,763 --> 00:12:58,024
[SPEAKER_04]: Why people can't see that?

154
00:12:59,365 --> 00:13:05,629
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would that require so much explanation as it does, and people would still do it wrongly?

155
00:13:05,689 --> 00:13:08,351
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the... where's the issue?

156
00:13:09,432 --> 00:13:24,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because you're so absorbed in your sense world, senses, unrestrained, never have any self-reflection, never pause, just going with whatever feels good, avoiding whatever feels bad.

157
00:13:25,369 --> 00:13:27,090
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you see your mind?

158
00:13:27,230 --> 00:13:32,332
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you step back to see your intentions?

159
00:13:32,352 --> 00:13:41,016
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but, like, even the person, if they're not restrained, and you tell them about yonisumanasikāra, the origin of your intentions, they can get their head around.

160
00:13:41,917 --> 00:13:48,820
[SPEAKER_04]: But why that, whatever they get their head around, why that will not be yonisumanasikāra?

161
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:50,861
[SPEAKER_04]: Why that will not be the origin?

162
00:13:53,963 --> 00:13:54,743
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be abstract.

163
00:13:56,276 --> 00:13:56,537
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure.

164
00:13:56,677 --> 00:13:56,897
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

165
00:13:57,177 --> 00:13:58,158
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would it be abstract?

166
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:03,522
[SPEAKER_04]: So, let's just stick with the origin of your action.

167
00:14:03,642 --> 00:14:11,489
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it needs to be attended properly.

168
00:14:11,729 --> 00:14:12,510
[SPEAKER_04]: Let's say it like that.

169
00:14:14,451 --> 00:14:24,319
[SPEAKER_04]: And when people think, if people have not been sense restrained, guarded of the sense doors, when they think they're attending it properly, they're not.

170
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:24,880
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

171
00:14:27,415 --> 00:14:30,056
[SPEAKER_04]: They're turning it into an object.

172
00:14:30,076 --> 00:14:31,437
[SPEAKER_04]: That's exactly the point.

173
00:14:31,797 --> 00:14:35,699
[SPEAKER_04]: They basically attend to it as another object of their senses, even thoughts.

174
00:14:36,759 --> 00:14:37,700
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's not what it is.

175
00:14:41,701 --> 00:14:51,186
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they cannot see anything more refined than object of what I'm attending, because that's where the mind is.

176
00:14:51,426 --> 00:14:53,807
[SPEAKER_04]: Mind takes that as a threshold of what is.

177
00:14:55,107 --> 00:14:59,251
[SPEAKER_04]: And mind takes that as a threshold of what is, because the lack of the sense restraint.

178
00:14:59,692 --> 00:15:03,275
[SPEAKER_04]: And when I say lack of virtue and sense restraint, I mean properly, done properly.

179
00:15:04,256 --> 00:15:07,059
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, endured properly, sufficiently.

180
00:15:07,079 --> 00:15:14,706
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's easy for people to be virtuous when they adopt the external set of circumstances, tradition, community, and so on.

181
00:15:14,907 --> 00:15:18,310
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, you just stick to the external behavior, but you haven't taken on the virtue.

182
00:15:19,536 --> 00:15:25,338
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why the factor of sīlabata paramāsa is one of the first three to go, if you start doing things rightly.

183
00:15:25,798 --> 00:15:37,221
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you have taken it on, in a sense of you are enduring doubt, you are still questioning the intentions behind your action, you don't just not do or do because the rules or the tradition says so.

184
00:15:38,402 --> 00:15:48,385
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if things are endured on the right level, then yes, your mind will cease to maintain the notion that the only thing that is real, the only thing that exists, is the one that I can attend to or access through my senses.

185
00:15:49,793 --> 00:15:55,056
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yoniso, meaning womb, manasikāra means the origin of your attention.

186
00:15:55,736 --> 00:15:57,717
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whatever you choose to attend, i.e.

187
00:15:57,797 --> 00:16:01,980
[SPEAKER_04]: direct your attention towards, yoniso of it is at the back of it.

188
00:16:03,080 --> 00:16:05,182
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's where this attention is coming from.

189
00:16:05,502 --> 00:16:10,925
[SPEAKER_04]: And you cannot attend that, in the same sense as you said before, an eye cannot see itself.

190
00:16:11,669 --> 00:16:12,450
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just impossible.

191
00:16:12,510 --> 00:16:18,235
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, the knowledge that the eye is there as a prerequisite for seeing, can be developed.

192
00:16:18,815 --> 00:16:20,197
[SPEAKER_04]: As a present absence.

193
00:16:20,877 --> 00:16:21,238
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

194
00:16:21,838 --> 00:16:28,524
[SPEAKER_04]: It is simultaneously present, but it can never be directly present as an object of your attention.

195
00:16:28,604 --> 00:16:31,387
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, it is present as a basis for your attention.

196
00:16:32,172 --> 00:16:33,193
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's that subtlety.

197
00:16:33,653 --> 00:16:47,000
[SPEAKER_04]: That, again, you can reason, and you can abstract it, and you can get your head around it, but unless the mind has been developed through correct endurance first, it will be just another thing you attend to.

198
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:49,961
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it won't be the yoniso of your manasikāra.

199
00:16:51,182 --> 00:17:00,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to kind of describe that in a practical way, in a way which isn't abstract, one could say it's like an absence of

200
00:17:00,890 --> 00:17:20,569
