364
00:29:07,049 --> 00:29:14,631
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you could maybe theoretically kind of differentiate what comes first, but overall, they arise together, they exist together, in that sense.

365
00:29:15,791 --> 00:29:18,491
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it wouldn't be one leads to the other that leads to the third one.

366
00:29:18,711 --> 00:29:23,873
[SPEAKER_04]: They're just there, because they're kind of three aspects of the same externally projected attitude.

367
00:29:24,293 --> 00:29:26,073
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is the in common to all three.

368
00:29:26,673 --> 00:29:30,974
[SPEAKER_04]: The external assumption of yourself, as in yourself exist independent of this experience as a whole,

369
00:29:32,734 --> 00:29:42,221
[SPEAKER_04]: The external dependence on the sets of norms, behaviors, authorities, not because you chose to do so and then feeling the weight and the uncertainty of your choice.

370
00:29:43,082 --> 00:29:47,325
[SPEAKER_04]: And, of course, the external engagement in order to prevent the pain of doubt.

371
00:29:49,907 --> 00:29:57,413
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you start uprooting this external assumption on any of these three hindrances, the other two will be uprooted as well.

372
00:29:57,633 --> 00:29:58,433
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, fetters, sorry.

373
00:29:58,914 --> 00:30:00,415
[SPEAKER_04]: The other two will be uprooted as well.

374
00:30:03,080 --> 00:30:09,982
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that would be the difference between somebody who is experiencing doubt, without being fettered by it, and somebody who is fettered by it.

375
00:30:10,742 --> 00:30:23,066
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the one who is not fettered by it, means he has endured it long enough, so that his mind has calmed down within that kicking and screaming of trying to get rid of the existential doubt, that's the most unpleasant thing.

376
00:30:24,266 --> 00:30:30,428
[SPEAKER_04]: And once it calms down within kicking and screaming, by not acting out of it, you then get to see it as an enduring phenomenon.

377
00:30:31,663 --> 00:30:35,984
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as unpleasant as it is, you see that it has arisen and thus it has to cease.

378
00:30:37,344 --> 00:30:43,986
[SPEAKER_04]: And your clarification of it, engagement, seeking the answer just to get rid of the pain of it, would just be to cover up.

379
00:30:44,026 --> 00:30:44,566
[SPEAKER_04]: And you know that.

380
00:30:44,966 --> 00:30:46,087
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you won't be fettered by it.

381
00:30:46,747 --> 00:30:49,307
[SPEAKER_04]: You'll still be pressured by it, hence the hindrance of doubt.

382
00:30:50,788 --> 00:30:58,990
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you still need to train your mind on more subtler levels to not act out, to not welcome and so on, but you're not fettered because you cannot adopt that view again.

383
00:30:59,898 --> 00:31:08,865
[SPEAKER_04]: Putting your getting rid of the pain first, as a valuable, as a valid thing, and thinking doubt just came externally, and if I address it... No, you realize... No.

384
00:31:09,205 --> 00:31:20,874
[SPEAKER_04]: Any direction of my effort towards external circumstances, in order to deal with the doubt, cannot be conceived anymore, because you don't... you understood that... you see the doubt doesn't come from external world.

385
00:31:22,295 --> 00:31:23,856
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why would you be addressing it there?

386
00:31:24,716 --> 00:31:29,520
[SPEAKER_04]: And people who have been addressing it there, is the one who haven't seen where it came from, because they haven't endured it long enough.

387
00:31:31,154 --> 00:31:39,237
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that kind of comes back to, you know, I must, I'm sure we said it before, the whole, like, sotāpati and the three fetters.

388
00:31:39,377 --> 00:31:47,200
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, you need to know, you know, what the Buddha taught, what, you know, clarify what the what the fetters are, what the ātavāda is, impermanence and so on.

389
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:50,021
[SPEAKER_04]: But fundamentally, people don't get the right view.

390
00:31:50,361 --> 00:31:54,283
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because they don't have the enough information and utterance of another.

391
00:31:56,048 --> 00:32:02,574
[SPEAKER_04]: In terms of what the right view is, they don't get it, because they haven't endured things sufficiently long enough on that level.

392
00:32:02,914 --> 00:32:05,096
[SPEAKER_04]: That's usually taken for granted.

393
00:32:05,156 --> 00:32:10,201
[SPEAKER_04]: It must be avoided, must be prevented, must be managed, must be covered up, must be dealt with.

394
00:32:11,602 --> 00:32:14,525
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why, as we said before, that's why yoniso manasikāra is not developed.

395
00:32:14,565 --> 00:32:17,787
[SPEAKER_04]: Things are not endured rightly, on the right level.

396
00:32:18,924 --> 00:32:19,704
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, here's a question.

397
00:32:20,044 --> 00:32:29,026
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose somebody wants to deliberately move towards that fetter of doubt by keeping the question, who am I, alive and making that as the main practice.

398
00:32:29,387 --> 00:32:31,907
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that a valid approach?

399
00:32:32,207 --> 00:32:33,107
[SPEAKER_04]: It could be, yeah.

400
00:32:33,247 --> 00:32:35,328
[SPEAKER_04]: It depends on what other things that person does.

401
00:32:37,148 --> 00:32:47,171
[SPEAKER_04]: Do they do that cultivation of that attitude paired with with sense restraint, guarding of the sense doors,

402
00:32:48,693 --> 00:32:53,836
[SPEAKER_04]: Not acting out towards other hindrances as well, and then keep themselves in question.

403
00:32:54,796 --> 00:32:55,196
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, yeah.

404
00:32:56,517 --> 00:33:02,460
[SPEAKER_04]: Then it will probably be like one of those ways that we described as quick and unpleasant.

405
00:33:04,581 --> 00:33:10,004
[SPEAKER_03]: But they're also seen in certain discourses as questions not to be pursued.

406
00:33:10,444 --> 00:33:12,685
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, am I, am I not, who am I, and so on.

407
00:33:13,113 --> 00:33:17,775
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no, see, not to be pursued if you still have a view that the answer will resolve the question.

408
00:33:18,035 --> 00:33:21,976
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, so that is the difference between then dealing with the fact... Yeah, you are not seeking for the answer.

409
00:33:22,236 --> 00:33:23,817
[SPEAKER_04]: You just want to cultivate the question...

410
00:33:23,837 --> 00:33:24,157
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

411
00:33:24,317 --> 00:33:28,738
[SPEAKER_04]: ...so that it reveals the underlying pain you're trying to get rid of and endure it.

412
00:33:30,319 --> 00:33:35,301
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, I mean, am I liable to future unwholesome states?

413
00:33:36,321 --> 00:33:36,941
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a question.

414
00:33:36,981 --> 00:33:37,781
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a valid question.

415
00:33:37,821 --> 00:33:38,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

416
00:33:40,827 --> 00:33:47,039
[SPEAKER_04]: Many other questions that would revolve even around the Atavāda and so on, would be valid questions, if you are not

417
00:33:48,109 --> 00:33:51,652
[SPEAKER_04]: As I said, seeking the answer to get rid of the discomfort of it.

418
00:33:52,313 --> 00:34:01,321
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're asking a question for the sake of cultivating the attitude, yeah, that's that's the whole practice of, you know, why am I doing what I'm doing, whether my intention is rooted.

419
00:34:01,461 --> 00:34:03,763
[SPEAKER_04]: It all begins with a question and stays within the question.

420
00:34:04,424 --> 00:34:13,552
[SPEAKER_04]: But the difference is whether you can bear it to stay on the level of the question without giving in to the temptation of, oh, but if I just get the clarification, just get the answer.

421
00:34:14,858 --> 00:34:15,940
[SPEAKER_03]: Then everything is going to be fine.

422
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:17,903
[SPEAKER_04]: Then things will fall into place and then... No, no.

423
00:34:17,923 --> 00:34:21,528
[SPEAKER_04]: The practice is to not overshoot it and not ignore it.

424
00:34:21,708 --> 00:34:22,510
[SPEAKER_04]: I undershoot it.

425
00:34:23,371 --> 00:34:28,238
[SPEAKER_04]: And, yeah, that practice begins with that uncertainty of your own questionable existence.

426
00:34:28,539 --> 00:34:29,280
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the whole point.

427
00:34:30,684 --> 00:34:39,031
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly why people would become dependent on external circumstances in terms of virtue, getting rid of doubt, even in terms of your own view of yourself.

428
00:34:40,112 --> 00:34:47,978
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because you overshot the mark for so long, so many times, you develop... that's became a threshold now, of what is, as I said before.

429
00:34:53,530 --> 00:35:00,994
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's like when Yanavera said, it's not about finding answer to these existential questions, it's about becoming the existential questions.

430
00:35:01,794 --> 00:35:04,475
[SPEAKER_04]: So, enduring them until you get used to it.

431
00:35:04,996 --> 00:35:11,379
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, the pain of the existential questioning will go away, because it was only conditioned by your wanting to get rid of it.

432
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:14,020
[SPEAKER_04]: But the question remains.

433
00:35:14,220 --> 00:35:18,982
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you realize, well, the question of this existence is the nature of existence.

434
00:35:19,063 --> 00:35:19,803
[SPEAKER_04]: It's uncertain.

435
00:35:21,846 --> 00:35:28,107
[SPEAKER_04]: Cannot be answered in those positive terms that you're used to operating on account of your existence.

436
00:35:28,127 --> 00:35:31,528
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like, you know, existence precedes the essence.

437
00:35:32,808 --> 00:35:34,249
[SPEAKER_04]: It just has to be that way.

438
00:35:39,350 --> 00:35:41,990
[SPEAKER_04]: So, another slightly related but somewhat different question.

439
00:35:42,190 --> 00:35:43,150
[SPEAKER_04]: We spoke about it before.

440
00:35:43,430 --> 00:35:44,431
[SPEAKER_04]: I thought now we can actually...

441
00:35:45,011 --> 00:35:46,151
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what's the difference then?

442
00:35:47,351 --> 00:35:47,911
[SPEAKER_04]: Or rather, no.

443
00:35:47,931 --> 00:35:48,652
[SPEAKER_04]: What comes first?

444
00:35:49,292 --> 00:35:50,312
[SPEAKER_04]: Hindrances or fetters?

445
00:35:57,506 --> 00:35:59,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Hindrance.

446
00:36:00,707 --> 00:36:12,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you were saying there's this fundamental wrong view that the fetter of doubt implies.

447
00:36:14,153 --> 00:36:20,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And by uprooting that, or by seeing that wrong view, one still is liable to hindrance.

448
00:36:20,955 --> 00:36:24,697
[SPEAKER_04]: But not to the full extent of the fetter.

449
00:36:24,837 --> 00:36:25,457
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

450
00:36:26,950 --> 00:36:30,437
[SPEAKER_04]: The hindrances are, on a practical level, more fundamental.

451
00:36:31,479 --> 00:36:38,292
[SPEAKER_04]: It's by not having your mind developed in regard to hindrances, all these fetters exist, that fetter you.

452
00:36:41,941 --> 00:37:00,413
[SPEAKER_04]: And the reason why I bring this up is because it is quite commonly assumed other way around, whereby you think these factors are like these mystical things that will fall off when you have these deep insights of your samādhi or whatever, and hindrances are kind of come and go, and as long as you're not lustful or angry or doubtful, you're free from them.

453
00:37:00,473 --> 00:37:01,054
[SPEAKER_04]: Actually, you're not.

454
00:37:01,434 --> 00:37:09,159
[SPEAKER_04]: Liability, as we discovered in other talks, liability to hindrances, so liability to lust arising, means you're still within the domain of lust.

455
00:37:09,439 --> 00:37:10,460
[SPEAKER_04]: You have not surmounted it.

456
00:37:11,314 --> 00:37:14,095
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, if you surmount one of them properly, you surmounted all five.

457
00:37:14,295 --> 00:37:15,375
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the first jhāna.

458
00:37:22,338 --> 00:37:23,258
[SPEAKER_04]: Because people do.

459
00:37:23,338 --> 00:37:33,102
[SPEAKER_04]: And I heard even, like, teachings being sort of taught by saying, well, no, you just look, like, in terms of lust, for example, like, most of the time, you're not lustful.

460
00:37:34,763 --> 00:37:36,503
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, your practice is going well.

461
00:37:36,683 --> 00:37:38,244
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, no, that's not the measure.

462
00:37:39,465 --> 00:37:44,088
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, a person in coma is most of the time not lustful, because he's unconscious.

463
00:37:44,628 --> 00:37:46,850
[SPEAKER_04]: Does that mean he's going towards enlightenment?

464
00:37:46,950 --> 00:37:47,110
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

465
00:37:49,071 --> 00:37:54,194
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can be most of the time not lustful or angry, but could lust arise.

466
00:37:55,295 --> 00:38:00,518
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're uncertain in regard to that, means you're well within the domain of lust, domain of the hindrances.

467
00:38:02,020 --> 00:38:07,963
[SPEAKER_04]: Because by same logic, then, you could say, well, most of the time, you're not engaging in a sexual intercourse.

468
00:38:08,423 --> 00:38:13,946
[SPEAKER_04]: And when you do, even such engagement doesn't last as long as most of the time when you're not engaging.

469
00:38:13,986 --> 00:38:15,567
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, most of the time, you're celibate.

470
00:38:16,401 --> 00:38:17,802
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not how celibacy works.

471
00:38:18,402 --> 00:38:20,404
[SPEAKER_04]: Most of the time, you don't kill anybody.

472
00:38:20,424 --> 00:38:23,106
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you were to kill somebody, it would last for a second only.

473
00:38:23,966 --> 00:38:26,848
[SPEAKER_04]: So, most of the time, you're keeping the precepts of not killing.

474
00:38:26,928 --> 00:38:27,729
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it doesn't matter.

475
00:38:28,129 --> 00:38:29,830
[SPEAKER_04]: Could I kill somebody?

476
00:38:30,371 --> 00:38:32,052
[SPEAKER_04]: Could I engage in sensuality?

477
00:38:32,172 --> 00:38:32,452
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

478
00:38:33,253 --> 00:38:33,633
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

479
00:38:34,654 --> 00:38:36,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Your mind is then within the hindrances.

480
00:38:39,148 --> 00:38:46,032
[SPEAKER_04]: So, most of the time you shouldn't be doing it, and while you're not doing it, you should then start looking at the liability of doing it.

481
00:38:46,052 --> 00:38:46,792
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, could I do it?

482
00:38:46,872 --> 00:38:48,493
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, that is a problem.

483
00:38:49,874 --> 00:38:51,074
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no safety there.

484
00:38:52,695 --> 00:39:04,762
[SPEAKER_04]: But if we measure whether there is a problem or not by the duration of how long you do it, well, you can get away with everything then, because, you know, most of those things wouldn't last as long as, you know, not doing those things.

485
00:39:09,290 --> 00:39:11,131
[SPEAKER_04]: Say, you have never heard about a Buddhist teaching.

486
00:39:11,972 --> 00:39:20,937
[SPEAKER_04]: But, for whatever reason, or through whatever teaching, you are actually practicing towards freedom from the liability to five hindrances.

487
00:39:21,758 --> 00:39:25,740
[SPEAKER_04]: Not just whether they're actually present or not, you are surmounting them as a whole.

488
00:39:25,860 --> 00:39:27,301
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you're developing the jhāna.

489
00:39:27,661 --> 00:39:27,861
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

490
00:39:28,942 --> 00:39:33,405
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, in your case, without any instruction of the Buddhist teaching,

491
00:39:34,325 --> 00:39:38,747
[SPEAKER_04]: Even the first three fetters, let's say like that, won't be as fettering.

492
00:39:39,868 --> 00:39:45,991
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the mind is not fueling them anymore through wrong attitude and acting out of hindrances.

493
00:39:48,252 --> 00:40:02,719
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly how in the suttas, some monks or people or like even followers of other practices, met the Buddha, heard one sentence and became like, boom, sotāpāna, anāgāmī, instantaneously.

494
00:40:04,168 --> 00:40:07,411
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because the mind has been developed beforehand, thoroughly.

495
00:40:07,711 --> 00:40:09,853
[SPEAKER_04]: They just needed somebody to put the pieces together.

496
00:40:09,913 --> 00:40:14,898
[SPEAKER_04]: Pieces that they have cultivated and developed and formed.

497
00:40:18,123 --> 00:40:24,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, the Buddha, after his awakening, considered who would be the first ones to understand this dhamma, which I have discovered.

498
00:40:24,668 --> 00:40:27,190
[SPEAKER_03]: And it was his two teachers who actually taught him.

499
00:40:27,610 --> 00:40:29,071
[SPEAKER_04]: Neither perception, no perception.

500
00:40:29,551 --> 00:40:30,572
[SPEAKER_03]: And the base of nothingness.

501
00:40:30,732 --> 00:40:31,152
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

502
00:40:31,553 --> 00:40:38,037
[SPEAKER_04]: So imagine, imagine if the first, just a measly first jhāna, surmounted all the hindrances.

503
00:40:38,878 --> 00:40:41,319
[SPEAKER_04]: An outsider develops the mind to that extent.

504
00:40:41,799 --> 00:40:47,802
[SPEAKER_04]: Imagine how much more the mind of somebody who has developed neither perception nor perception outside of the Buddha's teaching.

505
00:40:47,922 --> 00:40:56,926
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, as the Buddha himself said, oh, it's such a great loss for them that they died before they heard my teaching, because they virtually had no dust in their eyes.

506
00:40:56,946 --> 00:40:57,706
[SPEAKER_04]: They were so close.

507
00:40:57,906 --> 00:40:59,147
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, they would have seen this very quickly.

508
00:40:59,187 --> 00:41:01,108
[SPEAKER_04]: They would have probably not even finished the sentence.

509
00:41:01,148 --> 00:41:03,169
[SPEAKER_04]: They would have known exactly what he's talking about.

510
00:41:04,849 --> 00:41:06,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the work has been done.

511
00:41:09,609 --> 00:41:24,076
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why even throughout the day, if you're surmounting the hindrances, by not acting out, by enduring, by recognizing the present liability of hindrances...

512
00:41:24,096 --> 00:41:25,756
[SPEAKER_04]: So, see, they are not directly present.

513
00:41:25,796 --> 00:41:31,399
[SPEAKER_04]: You could say, oh, lust is absent, anger is absent, but it is present as a possibility.

514
00:41:31,979 --> 00:41:34,500
[SPEAKER_04]: And you need to start to see that and endure that.

515
00:41:36,701 --> 00:41:38,242
[SPEAKER_03]: You are actually practicing towards jhāna.

516
00:41:38,728 --> 00:41:41,309
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if you don't sit cross-legged and watch your nostrils.

517
00:41:41,510 --> 00:41:44,211
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, especially if you don't sit cross-legged and watch your nostrils.

518
00:41:45,652 --> 00:41:51,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, but you see, the possibility of something means that one needs to become sensitive to the, let's say, negative aspect of experience.

519
00:41:51,995 --> 00:41:54,557
[SPEAKER_03]: Something isn't happening, but could be happening.

520
00:41:54,917 --> 00:41:55,277
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

521
00:41:55,457 --> 00:41:57,658
[SPEAKER_04]: And how does one become sensitive to that?

522
00:41:58,399 --> 00:42:04,162
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you become sensitive to that by practicing sensitivity of bodily sensations and concentration?

523
00:42:05,071 --> 00:42:11,194
[SPEAKER_04]: Could you, let's say like that, could you become sensitive to that by doing meditation techniques and focusing practices?

524
00:42:17,017 --> 00:42:22,299
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that wouldn't be... that wouldn't be the main thing that makes that happen.

525
00:42:22,639 --> 00:42:23,580
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, no.

526
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:24,140
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

527
00:42:24,400 --> 00:42:24,580
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

528
00:42:25,561 --> 00:42:30,723
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, you know, any such practice as we just established is on the level of attending to objects.

529
00:42:33,059 --> 00:42:38,262
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means all you're going to be training yourself is attending to more specific objects within objects.

530
00:42:38,583 --> 00:42:39,703
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is not on that level.

531
00:42:40,104 --> 00:42:40,504
[SPEAKER_04]: That's right.

532
00:42:40,984 --> 00:42:42,945
[SPEAKER_04]: So, first, you need to be able to endure it.

533
00:42:43,166 --> 00:42:48,289
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's how you will become more sensitive to those things, to those liabilities.

534
00:42:49,570 --> 00:42:56,674
[SPEAKER_04]: By not acting out, by enduring, by not trying to get rid of the discomfort of pressure to act out, but by enduring it and not acting out.

535
00:42:57,074 --> 00:42:59,316
[SPEAKER_04]: So, not covering it up and not acting out.

536
00:43:00,216 --> 00:43:02,558
[SPEAKER_04]: The result is, oh, you're going to become very sensitive.

537
00:43:03,626 --> 00:43:06,688
[SPEAKER_04]: You're gonna see it even before you feel it.

538
00:43:06,728 --> 00:43:08,970
[SPEAKER_04]: So, now you're very discerning of it.

539
00:43:09,530 --> 00:43:11,311
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's that's exactly the practice.

540
00:43:12,552 --> 00:43:23,420
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is also how dwelling bodily secluded, not having other people around, that give a sort of confirmation, like I exist, I'm this way, that way, and so on, to not

541
00:43:23,883 --> 00:43:32,429
[SPEAKER_03]: We don't talk much, because most of the time, talking also has this sort of, let us say, interpersonal domain of wanting to be acknowledged, wanting to be understood, and so on.

542
00:43:32,989 --> 00:43:37,693
[SPEAKER_03]: This can easily feed into this whole assumption of self, and this is what I am, and this is how I'm seen.

543
00:43:38,233 --> 00:43:47,700
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you just get out of that situation, it, well, not necessarily by itself, but it certainly moves into the direction of having this sort of background uncertainty,

544
00:43:48,882 --> 00:44:01,210
[SPEAKER_03]: around your existence, become more salient, put a sensory strength on top of that, and the whole situation basically begins to sort of cook and boil a bit, without needing to make any effort positively in order to get somewhere.

545
00:44:01,470 --> 00:44:02,010
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

546
00:44:02,250 --> 00:44:04,052
[SPEAKER_04]: This becomes the positive effort.

547
00:44:04,232 --> 00:44:04,552
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

548
00:44:04,612 --> 00:44:05,673
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is basically negative.

549
00:44:05,733 --> 00:44:06,333
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

550
00:44:06,413 --> 00:44:16,159
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, protecting the certain circumstances, recognizing what it would be detrimental, and then avoiding it before you do it, that's your inverted comma positive effort.

551
00:44:16,219 --> 00:44:16,580
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

552
00:44:16,820 --> 00:44:16,980
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

553
00:44:18,692 --> 00:44:25,274
[SPEAKER_04]: But from the point of view of what's usually assumed to be the positive effort of practice, yeah, this is a negative effort of practice.

554
00:44:25,294 --> 00:44:29,515
[SPEAKER_04]: This is stuff you don't do, so that your practice arises as a result of it.

555
00:44:29,875 --> 00:44:35,577
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and then the inverted situation of that would be to sit with a group of people focusing on the tip of your nose.

556
00:44:36,217 --> 00:44:36,697
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

557
00:44:36,737 --> 00:44:38,598
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a very much specific thing you do.

558
00:44:38,978 --> 00:44:39,178
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

559
00:44:41,504 --> 00:44:57,352
[SPEAKER_04]: And just generally the... even say... even if you're not engaging with other... with company, just... and if you... if you... like, you know, if you're free from atavāda and so on, so there is no perpetuation of such assumptions and validations and so on.

560
00:44:57,693 --> 00:45:02,135
[SPEAKER_04]: Still, engaging with others, talking, company, is distracting on its own.

561
00:45:04,396 --> 00:45:04,756
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

562
00:45:04,776 --> 00:45:06,737
[SPEAKER_04]: It takes away the mind from itself.

563
00:45:06,937 --> 00:45:07,978
[SPEAKER_04]: Even in neutral terms.

564
00:45:08,078 --> 00:45:09,839
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if it's a neutral type of engagement.

565
00:45:10,922 --> 00:45:12,423
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why solitude is necessary.

566
00:45:12,663 --> 00:45:14,104
[SPEAKER_04]: Physical solitude is necessary.

567
00:45:18,266 --> 00:45:36,316
[SPEAKER_00]: One would think, eh... being among people could be a good exercise to, eh... be able to control, not control, but to be able to, eh... deal with these things.

568
00:45:36,376 --> 00:45:37,476
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean... Well, that's the whole point.

569
00:45:37,496 --> 00:45:38,677
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not supposed to deal with them.

570
00:45:39,117 --> 00:45:40,158
[SPEAKER_04]: You're supposed to uproot them.

571
00:45:41,084 --> 00:45:42,315
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not about learning how to deal.

572
00:45:43,665 --> 00:45:51,429
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, if you want to learn how to deal, being with people, you know, getting a psychotherapy degree, all of that, it's probably going to be helpful, your management.

573
00:45:52,110 --> 00:45:53,991
[SPEAKER_04]: But dhamma is not a management, it's uprooting.

574
00:45:54,811 --> 00:45:56,392
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's the question you should ask yourself.

575
00:45:56,632 --> 00:45:57,953
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I want to deal with these things?

576
00:45:58,513 --> 00:46:00,374
[SPEAKER_04]: Or do I just want to uproot them once and for all?

577
00:46:01,174 --> 00:46:11,700
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, more drastic measures need to be taken, such as not relying on people and not relying on learning the skill of management, because that requires to be affected first.

578
00:46:12,529 --> 00:46:25,264
[SPEAKER_00]: But one could say, okay, I'm secluded, I don't talk to anybody, and now I see... it appears that, you know, I have...

579
00:46:26,706 --> 00:46:28,028
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not acting out.

580
00:46:28,148 --> 00:46:31,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it... if you are with other people, you can...

581
00:46:33,261 --> 00:46:35,264
[SPEAKER_00]: They can do something, you could...

582
00:46:35,344 --> 00:46:35,564
[SPEAKER_04]: Right.

583
00:46:35,724 --> 00:46:48,560
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, see, that's the problem there, is that you can't see whether you're liable to greed, aversion, delusion, without external help and circumstances prodding you and pushing you, which means you have not developed what needs to be developed.

584
00:46:50,538 --> 00:47:00,583
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, circumstances can remind you of these things, but if you need circumstances to remind you, means you are not seeing the liability that doesn't need circumstances.

585
00:47:00,603 --> 00:47:01,344
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the whole point.

586
00:47:02,084 --> 00:47:07,107
[SPEAKER_04]: You are liable to be affected by things when you are not being affected by them directly.

587
00:47:07,827 --> 00:47:20,174
[SPEAKER_04]: But if the only way for you to recognize that you are liable is to be affected in the first place, means, phew, more work and sensory strength will be required, because, again, that's the level you need to really transcend and get beyond.

588
00:47:21,315 --> 00:47:23,557
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you want to be secluded and it will enough?

589
00:47:24,498 --> 00:47:28,742
[SPEAKER_04]: It should be enough for you to just ask yourself, can the lust arise?

590
00:47:29,870 --> 00:47:34,634
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you're unclear, that's enough for you to know that, yes, I am liable.

591
00:47:35,375 --> 00:47:39,879
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you train in regard to that question and in regard to that liability.

592
00:47:39,899 --> 00:47:41,521
[SPEAKER_04]: Not, oh, I don't know.

593
00:47:41,941 --> 00:47:46,265
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, let me go and seek the external lust to confirm that I could.

594
00:47:46,465 --> 00:47:49,068
[SPEAKER_04]: The fact is that you don't know means, yes, that's the answer.

595
00:47:49,128 --> 00:47:52,331
[SPEAKER_03]: You can be affected because you don't know whether you can or cannot be affected.

596
00:47:54,853 --> 00:47:55,854
[SPEAKER_04]: And for that person,

597
00:47:57,596 --> 00:48:05,224
[SPEAKER_04]: Being prompted by others... yeah, sure, somebody... Buddha can come and, out of compassion, tell you something more, and then, kind of, make you think better, and so on.

598
00:48:05,925 --> 00:48:15,035
[SPEAKER_04]: But, the way... well, at least the way I understood what you said initially, was being with others is good, because it will remind you of greed, aversion, delusion, and the work you need to do.

599
00:48:15,596 --> 00:48:18,419
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it will remind you of acute greed, aversion, delusion.

600
00:48:19,942 --> 00:48:25,209
[SPEAKER_04]: And the only reason that's possible is because you're already not seeing the liability of those things.

601
00:48:25,890 --> 00:48:28,274
[SPEAKER_04]: And for that, seclusion is necessary.

602
00:48:32,710 --> 00:48:34,071
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's quite a common view though.

603
00:48:34,231 --> 00:48:36,914
[SPEAKER_03]: People saying like, okay, I'm living together with someone.

604
00:48:36,934 --> 00:48:37,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, of course.

605
00:48:37,455 --> 00:48:41,198
[SPEAKER_03]: We are occasionally pushing one another's, like, pushing one another's buttons.

606
00:48:41,338 --> 00:48:42,399
[SPEAKER_03]: All of this is unpleasant.

607
00:48:43,120 --> 00:48:44,021
[SPEAKER_03]: I have to deal with it.

608
00:48:44,641 --> 00:48:46,343
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the practice.

609
00:48:46,503 --> 00:48:46,984
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

610
00:48:47,524 --> 00:48:56,973
[SPEAKER_03]: But even then, you know, having your buttons pressed, if you are half reasonable, you might develop some patience or endurance, but it is all within the domain of sensuality still.

611
00:48:57,013 --> 00:49:08,156
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, again, and even if you don't have anybody pressing your buttons, if you're honest with yourself, you could recognize that my buttons could be pressed, and that's enough for somebody who has developed the mind sufficiently to begin practicing.

612
00:49:08,216 --> 00:49:09,016
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but that's almost...

613
00:49:09,036 --> 00:49:11,576
[SPEAKER_04]: They could be pressed, means that's it.

614
00:49:11,816 --> 00:49:12,937
[SPEAKER_04]: That is a form of pressed.

615
00:49:13,097 --> 00:49:13,297
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

616
00:49:13,357 --> 00:49:19,638
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't need others external... Again, we come back to that requiring that certain assumed threshold of what is.

617
00:49:20,498 --> 00:49:24,039
[SPEAKER_04]: So, an actual last aversion delusion is what last aversion delusion is.

618
00:49:24,639 --> 00:49:26,340
[SPEAKER_04]: The only way to deal with liability

619
00:49:27,860 --> 00:49:32,703
[SPEAKER_04]: With possibility of last aversion delusion is to actively engage with last aversion delusion.

620
00:49:32,763 --> 00:49:32,983
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

621
00:49:33,103 --> 00:49:34,003
[SPEAKER_04]: It's other way around.

622
00:49:35,944 --> 00:49:38,785
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to stop engaging, so to see liability correctly.

623
00:49:39,306 --> 00:49:40,806
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you will have no need to engage.

624
00:49:41,627 --> 00:49:43,348
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

625
00:49:45,549 --> 00:49:49,170
[SPEAKER_04]: And there is a difference there, that we can now obviously differentiate.

626
00:49:49,871 --> 00:49:54,833
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, are you paired with another person in a sense of a teacher?

627
00:49:56,440 --> 00:50:03,384
[SPEAKER_04]: Who is not pushing your buttons in terms of lust, aversion, delusion, but giving instruction necessary for practice?

628
00:50:03,904 --> 00:50:10,067
[SPEAKER_04]: Or are you just paired with random strangers that you're just clashing with emotionally and so on, due to circumstances, history or whatever else?

629
00:50:10,588 --> 00:50:14,870
[SPEAKER_04]: Those are two very different... You can say, well, see, both are company, but they're very different types of company.

630
00:50:16,851 --> 00:50:20,593
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what, what was it, the rhinoceros says, the sutta.

631
00:50:21,294 --> 00:50:23,135
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't seek company just with anyone.

632
00:50:23,555 --> 00:50:25,296
[SPEAKER_04]: Only with equal or better than yourself.

633
00:50:26,642 --> 00:50:28,363
[SPEAKER_04]: And even then, it's for the purpose of learning.

634
00:50:29,944 --> 00:50:33,047
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's not for that purpose, that company should not be cultivated.

635
00:50:33,407 --> 00:50:42,313
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you might be stuck in certain companies, which you have to endure, but if you now start justifying that for your practice, that's... that's a cover-up.

636
00:50:42,473 --> 00:50:43,174
[SPEAKER_04]: That's a cover-up.

637
00:50:43,294 --> 00:50:43,594
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

638
00:50:45,376 --> 00:50:49,038
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, same or better in terms of virtue, composure and understanding.

639
00:50:49,118 --> 00:50:50,339
[SPEAKER_04]: And the views, yes, exactly.

640
00:50:50,399 --> 00:50:51,160
[SPEAKER_04]: Same or better.

641
00:50:51,400 --> 00:50:52,260
[SPEAKER_04]: Equal or higher.

642
00:50:52,461 --> 00:50:52,681
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

643
00:50:52,881 --> 00:50:53,081
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

644
00:50:54,682 --> 00:50:55,563
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, obviously, as I said,

645
00:50:57,494 --> 00:50:59,156
[SPEAKER_04]: Where are you going to find that today?

646
00:50:59,597 --> 00:51:01,359
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, how many people would seek that?

647
00:51:02,080 --> 00:51:08,247
[SPEAKER_04]: So you need to recognize, okay, the circumstances I'm in are not the most favorable, but let me at least not cover them up.

648
00:51:09,584 --> 00:51:16,187
[SPEAKER_04]: Endure the discomfort of it, and that might make rise to some other choices that I have not considered, that might be beneficial for my practice.

649
00:51:16,247 --> 00:51:33,114
[SPEAKER_04]: But those choices will not arise, if you try to justify your circumstances, cover them up, and kind of ignore what you know need to be done, such as, yeah, sense of strength, guarding the sense of seclusion, not engagement, solitude, discerning these liabilities, and so on.

650
00:51:33,794 --> 00:51:34,875
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, non-distraction.

651
00:51:39,620 --> 00:51:42,702
[SPEAKER_04]: So, do I spend... how do I spend my time throughout the day?

652
00:51:43,182 --> 00:51:49,085
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I engaging for the sake of company, for the sake of distraction, for the sake of boredom?

653
00:51:49,886 --> 00:51:52,647
[SPEAKER_04]: Again, household or not, it applies the same.

654
00:51:52,888 --> 00:51:54,308
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the same rooted motivation.

655
00:51:54,348 --> 00:51:55,189
[SPEAKER_04]: Where is it rooted in?

656
00:51:55,989 --> 00:52:02,093
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you might recognize, well, it's a bit too much to do this right away, but you mustn't cover it up.

657
00:52:02,453 --> 00:52:03,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, it's too much to do right away.

658
00:52:04,174 --> 00:52:05,835
[SPEAKER_04]: I must make myself able to do it.

659
00:52:06,877 --> 00:52:07,318
[SPEAKER_04]: Eventually.

660
00:52:07,719 --> 00:52:08,160
[SPEAKER_04]: Later on.

661
00:52:08,741 --> 00:52:10,424
[SPEAKER_04]: Not like, oh, it's too much now.

662
00:52:10,925 --> 00:52:12,388
[SPEAKER_04]: I can afford to not think about it.

663
00:52:12,668 --> 00:52:15,814
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, at the same time, entertain this notion that you are still practicing.

664
00:52:19,985 --> 00:52:29,675
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and then, there can also be the view that practice and study is about, in that sense, gaining or accumulating something, whereas, actually, it's about abandoning things.

665
00:52:29,776 --> 00:52:30,737
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about undoing, yeah.

666
00:52:30,817 --> 00:52:37,864
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about undoing rather than adding something, rather than keeping what is already going on, and then adding something to it.

667
00:52:38,605 --> 00:52:41,347
[SPEAKER_03]: Some sort of spiritual layer to your life, let's say.

668
00:52:41,607 --> 00:52:41,827
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

669
00:52:42,687 --> 00:52:54,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, actually, it's about taking things away from what you are already doing, which then will create more favorable circumstances and a sort of space to to see what's the next step, experientially.

670
00:52:55,174 --> 00:52:58,276
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you take away... Maybe somebody else can join.

671
00:52:58,296 --> 00:52:58,696
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know.

672
00:52:59,036 --> 00:53:02,758
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you take away those things that you know you need to take away?

673
00:53:04,319 --> 00:53:05,140
[SPEAKER_03]: Like what you just said.

674
00:53:06,260 --> 00:53:13,926
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, first of all, by seeing the danger in perpetuating those things... What would those things be, in broad terms?

675
00:53:14,406 --> 00:53:17,008
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, this is obviously the whole domain of sensuality.

676
00:53:17,429 --> 00:53:18,910
[SPEAKER_03]: Sensuality would be one of them, yeah.

677
00:53:20,234 --> 00:53:21,334
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that would be the main one.

678
00:53:21,734 --> 00:53:29,816
[SPEAKER_03]: And all... Because, you see, we can say sensuality, but it might not be clear what actually encompasses this whole domain of sensuality.

679
00:53:29,956 --> 00:53:31,057
[SPEAKER_03]: That's now another question.

680
00:53:31,097 --> 00:53:41,039
[SPEAKER_03]: It's easy to keep coming back to this point, but to just see where sensuality begins and ends, so to speak, already implies... That can be a separate talk.

681
00:53:41,299 --> 00:53:48,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, because, in a sense, that whole domain needs to have ceased in order for you to have the direct knowledge what is the extent of the domain.

682
00:53:48,241 --> 00:53:48,421
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

683
00:53:48,861 --> 00:53:49,121
[SPEAKER_03]: So...

684
00:53:50,624 --> 00:53:51,245
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's not going...

685
00:53:51,265 --> 00:53:58,211
[SPEAKER_04]: But if somebody who doesn't know the extent of their domain, but they want to start undoing things, as we said...

686
00:53:59,612 --> 00:54:02,094
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, of course, you know, you take on the precepts.

687
00:54:02,515 --> 00:54:02,735
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

688
00:54:04,537 --> 00:54:05,377
[SPEAKER_04]: Guard the sense doors.

689
00:54:07,039 --> 00:54:08,660
[SPEAKER_04]: Where else do you look for the undoing?

690
00:54:10,882 --> 00:54:13,284
[SPEAKER_04]: Where would you find internally what you need to undo?

691
00:54:14,711 --> 00:54:25,059
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if you don't have any specific information or you need to undo this or this or this, there is a certain thing you can find that's uniformly present in everything that you need to undo.

692
00:54:25,460 --> 00:54:28,782
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's a basic self-forgetfulness.

693
00:54:29,203 --> 00:54:30,904
[SPEAKER_03]: Like when somebody is doing something,

694
00:54:31,124 --> 00:54:43,713
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether it is going to a restaurant, being absorbed in food and pleasant circumstances, binge-watching television series and so on, what is pleasant about that is the unawareness of existence and the burden that comes with it.

695
00:54:44,133 --> 00:54:50,557
[SPEAKER_04]: What is in common to eating in a restaurant, pleasure of distraction, pleasure of forgetfulness?

696
00:54:51,858 --> 00:54:54,060
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the uncommon... what is a common trait?

697
00:54:56,121 --> 00:54:57,142
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not distraction, is it?

698
00:54:57,520 --> 00:54:57,961
[SPEAKER_04]: Dearness.

699
00:54:58,361 --> 00:54:58,641
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry?

700
00:54:58,882 --> 00:54:59,142
[SPEAKER_04]: Dearness.

701
00:54:59,162 --> 00:55:00,143
[SPEAKER_04]: Dearness, that's, yeah.

702
00:55:00,423 --> 00:55:02,365
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what's, what, like, things that are dear to you.

703
00:55:02,425 --> 00:55:03,346
[SPEAKER_04]: Why are they dear to you?

704
00:55:07,010 --> 00:55:12,496
[SPEAKER_04]: Anything that's dear, that you regard as friendly, including your own mind, as the sutta says, you should see it as an enemy, as an assassin.

705
00:55:13,116 --> 00:55:15,018
[SPEAKER_04]: Why are you regarding it as dear and friendly?

706
00:55:16,380 --> 00:55:17,221
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't see the danger.

707
00:55:18,138 --> 00:55:21,482
[SPEAKER_04]: And when something is not dangerous, how is it felt?

708
00:55:21,963 --> 00:55:23,565
[SPEAKER_04]: Pleasant.

709
00:55:23,605 --> 00:55:23,865
[SPEAKER_04]: Safe.

710
00:55:23,985 --> 00:55:24,246
[SPEAKER_04]: Safe.

711
00:55:24,846 --> 00:55:25,607
[SPEAKER_03]: It's that safety.

712
00:55:25,787 --> 00:55:27,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Which, of course, it's felt pleasant.

713
00:55:27,890 --> 00:55:32,075
[SPEAKER_04]: So, any safety you find in anything, you can start undermining that.

714
00:55:33,357 --> 00:55:34,658
[SPEAKER_04]: And that would be different things.

715
00:55:34,779 --> 00:55:37,061
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why there is no external set.

716
00:55:39,212 --> 00:55:47,237
[SPEAKER_04]: Some people will find safety in doing certain things, in certain habits, in access to certain things.

717
00:55:47,257 --> 00:55:48,618
[SPEAKER_04]: Some people will find something else.

718
00:55:48,998 --> 00:55:52,960
[SPEAKER_04]: What is in common is, you do find a sense of safety in it.

719
00:55:53,240 --> 00:55:53,681
[SPEAKER_04]: In something.

720
00:55:54,221 --> 00:55:57,443
[SPEAKER_04]: In doing something, in having something, in belonging to something.

721
00:55:58,790 --> 00:56:04,634
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you follow the thread of where you find safety and keep pulling it out, yeah, you are going in the right direction.

722
00:56:05,215 --> 00:56:12,299
[SPEAKER_04]: Because then there will be nothing that you find safe in this world and that's the true disenchantment and dispassion that will result in arahantship.

723
00:56:13,140 --> 00:56:21,246
[SPEAKER_04]: But see, in order to do that, you need to be able to endure such lack of safety without losing your mind or breaking down or distracting yourself.

724
00:56:22,138 --> 00:56:26,142
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but we're talking about safety here in the context of one's existence.

725
00:56:26,162 --> 00:56:29,185
[SPEAKER_03]: We're not talking about whether you put on your safety belt in the car or not.

726
00:56:29,545 --> 00:56:29,866
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

727
00:56:30,426 --> 00:56:30,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, no, no.

728
00:56:31,027 --> 00:56:34,090
[SPEAKER_03]: But I'm not saying, like, don't put on your seat belt in the car.

729
00:56:34,270 --> 00:56:34,991
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, right, right.

730
00:56:35,031 --> 00:56:35,431
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

731
00:56:35,451 --> 00:56:36,072
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

732
00:56:36,092 --> 00:56:42,378
[SPEAKER_04]: See, undoing the sense of... the thread of what... where you find safety in, does not mean exposing yourself to danger.

733
00:56:42,478 --> 00:56:42,818
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

734
00:56:42,838 --> 00:56:43,419
[SPEAKER_04]: That's my point.

735
00:56:43,439 --> 00:56:43,639
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

736
00:56:43,659 --> 00:56:43,819
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

737
00:56:44,440 --> 00:56:45,480
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, quite the opposite.

738
00:56:45,681 --> 00:56:46,221
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

739
00:56:46,261 --> 00:56:50,142
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you don't need to go external, extreme circumstances.

740
00:56:50,182 --> 00:56:59,766
[SPEAKER_04]: You just recognize there are things, see, because you are not an arahant, means you are resting upon something, upon some possessions, some value, some ideas.

741
00:57:00,567 --> 00:57:02,907
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, you are finding safety in them.

742
00:57:02,947 --> 00:57:04,508
[SPEAKER_04]: To that extent, you feel sheltered.

743
00:57:05,468 --> 00:57:06,349
[SPEAKER_04]: Start questioning that.

744
00:57:06,389 --> 00:57:08,330
[SPEAKER_04]: Start putting that in question, undermining it.

745
00:57:08,930 --> 00:57:13,612
[SPEAKER_04]: And there it is, the most acute practice you can have, if you can hold it.

746
00:57:14,434 --> 00:57:20,060
[SPEAKER_03]: That's actually one of the four summaries of the dhamma, that, you know, this world is without shelter and protection.

747
00:57:20,840 --> 00:57:21,601
[SPEAKER_03]: It literally says that.

748
00:57:22,782 --> 00:57:23,483
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, exactly.

749
00:57:23,523 --> 00:57:24,024
[SPEAKER_04]: Through and through.

750
00:57:24,224 --> 00:57:24,384
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

751
00:57:24,404 --> 00:57:25,185
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, there's nothing.

752
00:57:25,265 --> 00:57:27,547
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's similar in other sutras.

753
00:57:27,727 --> 00:57:33,133
[SPEAKER_04]: He realized there is nothing worth holding on, possessing, clinging to, whatever you want to translate it.

754
00:57:33,153 --> 00:57:33,373
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

755
00:57:34,114 --> 00:57:37,077
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, to that extent, you'll be undermined by it.

756
00:57:39,328 --> 00:58:03,371
[SPEAKER_04]: That was what you were telling the other day, that renunciation is getting out of the security... Like, even if you don't necessarily see all these, like, nuances, if you start keeping the precepts, like, rightly, not just on a level of external authority, but because you choose to do so, and you keep choosing to do so every single time, yeah, that renunciation is emulating that.

757
00:58:04,763 --> 00:58:10,025
[SPEAKER_04]: Basically, that undermining of things that you're taking for granted, that giving up, that relinquishment.

758
00:58:12,226 --> 00:58:18,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And renunciating the household life is even more, I mean, getting out of your security.

759
00:58:19,950 --> 00:58:20,610
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly, yeah.

760
00:58:20,650 --> 00:58:27,253
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, see, sometimes people can renounce immediate things that provide you with safety, but they cannot renounce

761
00:58:29,278 --> 00:58:34,002
[SPEAKER_04]: The need for maybe access to safety.

762
00:58:34,062 --> 00:58:39,027
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I'm not accessing it now, but I would like to have an option to access it if I need to.

763
00:58:39,968 --> 00:58:46,954
[SPEAKER_04]: So, to that extent, you are still not truly putting yourself in that position of uncertainty.

764
00:58:47,958 --> 00:58:54,924
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like, okay, yeah, I'm not engaging with this, but if push comes to shove, I could, and that in itself will offer you safety.

765
00:58:54,984 --> 00:59:02,892
[SPEAKER_04]: The possibility that you could engage with things that you used to engage with for the sake of safety and relief and pleasure and so on, that's enough.

766
00:59:02,972 --> 00:59:05,694
[SPEAKER_04]: That becomes then your safety, your relief, your pleasure.

767
00:59:06,134 --> 00:59:09,938
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not doing it, but I would if I'm pressed far enough.

768
00:59:11,421 --> 00:59:13,342
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's something you actually need to abandon.

769
00:59:14,122 --> 00:59:20,944
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to give up and just... just decide, no, I won't, even if my life depends on it, I won't engage with it.

770
00:59:22,264 --> 00:59:23,624
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it feels very differently.

771
00:59:24,145 --> 00:59:28,786
[SPEAKER_04]: It's kind of similar to what we spoke before, like, in terms of sensuality and giving up the addiction.

772
00:59:29,466 --> 00:59:34,507
[SPEAKER_04]: Try to give up cigarettes or similar, and then it's like, oh, yes, I'm not...

773
00:59:34,547 --> 00:59:35,728
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not smoking currently.

774
00:59:36,928 --> 00:59:39,649
[SPEAKER_04]: But if the need comes up, I could.

775
00:59:40,682 --> 00:59:41,903
[SPEAKER_04]: And they're fine with that idea.

776
00:59:41,923 --> 00:59:43,644
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's like, no, you shouldn't be fine with that idea.

777
00:59:43,664 --> 00:59:48,107
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you should even decide on the level of, if a need comes up, no, never again.

778
00:59:48,808 --> 00:59:50,029
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it feels very different.

779
00:59:50,049 --> 00:59:52,471
[SPEAKER_04]: Then the weight is much more serious.

780
00:59:52,511 --> 00:59:55,893
[SPEAKER_04]: And the Buddha gave the similar sutra, the simile of the quail.

781
00:59:57,234 --> 01:00:01,077
[SPEAKER_04]: He says, sometimes I make a little rule and say, monks, don't do that.

782
01:00:02,158 --> 01:00:02,718
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's a little.

783
01:00:02,738 --> 01:00:03,639
[SPEAKER_04]: It's insignificant.

784
01:00:04,119 --> 01:00:05,980
[SPEAKER_04]: And then some monks say, well, that's insignificant.

785
01:00:06,100 --> 01:00:06,680
[SPEAKER_04]: So they do it.

786
01:00:07,360 --> 01:00:09,241
[SPEAKER_04]: Then some other monks say, well, that's insignificant.

787
01:00:09,261 --> 01:00:10,662
[SPEAKER_04]: So they make the effort and abandon it.

788
01:00:11,242 --> 01:00:12,263
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's insignificant.

789
01:00:12,863 --> 01:00:13,623
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would you then do it?

790
01:00:14,044 --> 01:00:15,825
[SPEAKER_04]: And the Buddha said, the monks that...

791
01:00:16,325 --> 01:00:17,425
[SPEAKER_04]: It's truly insignificant.

792
01:00:18,066 --> 01:00:26,950
[SPEAKER_04]: But the ones that don't abandon it, they're like a quail that's tied down with insignificant rotten, half-rotten ropes.

793
01:00:27,470 --> 01:00:31,412
[SPEAKER_04]: But because it's a weak quail, those such ropes are enough to keep it bound.

794
01:00:32,479 --> 01:00:40,786
[SPEAKER_04]: The ones that abandon even the seemingly insignificant things are basically like elephants tied with leather straps.

795
01:00:41,427 --> 01:00:44,850
[SPEAKER_04]: But they would, because they're elephants, they would break the leather straps.

796
01:00:45,250 --> 01:00:49,233
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in other words, things feel insignificant because you're not abandoning them.

797
01:00:50,454 --> 01:00:56,940
[SPEAKER_04]: If you master the determination to say no in the ultimate sense, you realize they're leather straps, actually.

798
01:00:58,500 --> 01:01:00,682
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's easy to go, oh, no, no, this is just a rope.

799
01:01:00,742 --> 01:01:01,602
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just a rotten rope.

800
01:01:01,822 --> 01:01:02,603
[SPEAKER_04]: This is not a bond.

801
01:01:03,324 --> 01:01:05,345
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're not committing to abandoning it.

802
01:01:05,565 --> 01:01:07,487
[SPEAKER_04]: That's when it will be felt differently.

803
01:01:08,167 --> 01:01:10,649
[SPEAKER_04]: If you decide in that ultimate sense, never again.

804
01:01:11,650 --> 01:01:12,510
[SPEAKER_04]: Then you feel the weight.

805
01:01:12,730 --> 01:01:14,051
[SPEAKER_04]: Then you, oh, it's an actual strap.

806
01:01:14,392 --> 01:01:14,992
[SPEAKER_04]: A leather strap.

807
01:01:15,372 --> 01:01:21,437
[SPEAKER_04]: But because you have decided such thing and abstained from such actions, you become an elephant that can break even the leather straps.

808
01:01:36,148 --> 01:01:47,193
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, so by abandoning that possibility of coming back to it in the future, all the weight is, let us say, concentrated in the present, which is where the burden is fully felt.

809
01:01:47,653 --> 01:01:49,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, the burden of the entire extent.

810
01:01:50,195 --> 01:01:50,575
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

811
01:01:50,835 --> 01:01:57,198
[SPEAKER_04]: All the future possible, it doesn't matter now, it's the same determination covering it all, never again.

812
01:01:57,618 --> 01:02:02,404
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, but when that possibility is there, like, I might come back to it in the future, this is... That's it, that's a relief.

813
01:02:02,644 --> 01:02:14,938
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that precisely, so the burden in the present is not felt, because there is this sort of time interval, like, in the future somewhere, this could still exist for me, and consequently the problem is not seen now.

814
01:02:14,958 --> 01:02:18,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So you say you give up sensuality, or never

815
01:02:19,881 --> 01:02:25,585
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't use sensuality as a distraction or escape from the pain I feel, the agitation I feel.

816
01:02:25,665 --> 01:02:27,906
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I won't ever go in that direction again.

817
01:02:28,707 --> 01:02:28,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Ever.

818
01:02:30,208 --> 01:02:32,870
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you feel, now what do I...?

819
01:02:33,070 --> 01:02:33,290
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

820
01:02:34,271 --> 01:02:35,451
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I don't know what to do.

821
01:02:35,511 --> 01:02:38,934
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know what to do now, but I know what I will not do.

822
01:02:39,914 --> 01:02:40,295
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you feel...

823
01:02:40,315 --> 01:02:41,455
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then you have to endure it.

824
01:02:41,635 --> 01:02:42,016
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

825
01:02:42,176 --> 01:02:46,719
[SPEAKER_04]: Endure that lack of safety, but not compromise the direction you know you shouldn't go.

826
01:02:48,397 --> 01:02:51,778
[SPEAKER_04]: And once you endure it long enough, you get to see where to go and what to do.

827
01:02:51,938 --> 01:02:53,279
[SPEAKER_04]: And if there is anything you need to do.

828
01:02:53,659 --> 01:02:53,859
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

829
01:02:53,879 --> 01:02:55,500
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is this negative approach again.

830
01:02:55,540 --> 01:02:58,461
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not about finding something positively to move towards.

831
01:02:58,621 --> 01:03:01,062
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about abandoning that future possibility.

832
01:03:01,082 --> 01:03:01,302
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

833
01:03:02,022 --> 01:03:02,882
[SPEAKER_03]: And not sustaining that.

834
01:03:03,202 --> 01:03:03,483
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

835
01:03:04,963 --> 01:03:05,903
[SPEAKER_03]: That future possibility.

836
01:03:05,944 --> 01:03:12,606
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you get to see the dhamma if you basically remove all of your attitudes, views, preconceived notions that are covering it up.

837
01:03:13,766 --> 01:03:14,627
[SPEAKER_04]: Even the Buddha himself.

838
01:03:15,492 --> 01:03:16,532
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the only way we can do it.

839
01:03:16,552 --> 01:03:20,733
[SPEAKER_04]: He said, oh, it's like finding the ancient path that was covered and I uncovered it.

840
01:03:22,113 --> 01:03:28,375
[SPEAKER_04]: But you don't get to see the dhamma by doing practices on top of everything else, all the views and notions and preconceived ideas.

841
01:03:29,395 --> 01:03:32,956
[SPEAKER_04]: No, that's just piling up, piling up, piling up, piling up of wrong views.

842
01:03:34,636 --> 01:03:42,698
[SPEAKER_04]: If those things are not seen as a result of undoing all of your views, notions, habits and so on, it's going to be on the level of wrong practice.

843
01:03:45,327 --> 01:03:51,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any view that one holds is rooted in this sense of safety, basically.

844
01:03:51,672 --> 01:03:52,432
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, yeah.

845
01:03:52,592 --> 01:04:00,918
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, the right view begins by recognizing that the right practice is about undoing all of your safety stances, internally.

846
01:04:01,559 --> 01:04:06,943
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's still technically a view, but it's a view that actually undermines need for views.

847
01:04:07,623 --> 01:04:12,427
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's when arahant, eventually, when he becomes an arahant, is gone beyond the views.

848
01:04:13,477 --> 01:04:15,578
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even the right view is gone in him, so to speak.

849
01:04:17,759 --> 01:04:18,099
[SPEAKER_04]: Ceased.

850
01:04:18,520 --> 01:04:19,560
[SPEAKER_04]: No need for it anymore.

851
01:04:21,301 --> 01:04:22,682
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the views have been extinguished.

852
01:04:22,842 --> 01:04:23,722
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what the views are.

853
01:04:24,382 --> 01:04:25,423
[SPEAKER_04]: Standpoints of safety.

854
01:04:26,640 --> 01:04:28,382
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, we've touched on it last time.

855
01:04:28,462 --> 01:04:28,882
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

856
01:04:29,242 --> 01:04:29,422
[SPEAKER_02]: When?

857
01:04:29,643 --> 01:04:29,943
[SPEAKER_02]: We did.

858
01:04:30,143 --> 01:04:35,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we wanted to speak about it again, the safety, the pleasure that comes from views, of why you hold on to your views.

859
01:04:35,087 --> 01:04:35,708
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no, no, no.

860
01:04:35,848 --> 01:04:39,391
[SPEAKER_02]: You abandon sensuality, sensual pleasures.

861
01:04:39,551 --> 01:04:40,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

862
01:04:40,212 --> 01:04:41,433
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you start holding on to views.

863
01:04:41,453 --> 01:04:42,654
[SPEAKER_04]: The safety of the views, yeah.

864
01:04:43,075 --> 01:04:44,976
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, very much so.

865
01:04:45,056 --> 01:04:46,658
[SPEAKER_04]: The conviction, the certainty.

866
01:04:49,098 --> 01:04:50,639
[SPEAKER_02]: It protects you from pain.

867
01:04:51,260 --> 01:04:59,765
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why people double down in these views once they... And the suttas say that once samanas and brāhmaṇas withdraw from sensuality, then they start adhering, i.e.

868
01:05:00,406 --> 01:05:02,607
[SPEAKER_04]: doubling down even harder to the views.

869
01:05:03,047 --> 01:05:07,370
[SPEAKER_04]: And again, views not on the level of these metaphysical ideas of cosmos.

870
01:05:07,490 --> 01:05:07,610
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

871
01:05:07,670 --> 01:05:15,315
[SPEAKER_04]: Views on the level of you shouldn't be sitting like that, and he shouldn't be speaking like that, or how can he do this, how can they do that.

872
01:05:15,655 --> 01:05:16,416
[SPEAKER_04]: Those are the views.

873
01:05:17,276 --> 01:05:21,338
[SPEAKER_04]: All of those are the views, and that's exactly what Samanins and Brahmins fall out over.

874
01:05:22,839 --> 01:05:32,664
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, they live keeping the same rules, same environment, and then they absolutely kind of... their minds lose perspective on account of just these stance... standpoints.

875
01:05:32,704 --> 01:05:38,527
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I find it comforting and I find sense of safety if things are done this way.

876
01:05:39,387 --> 01:05:40,028
[SPEAKER_04]: That's my view.

877
01:05:40,963 --> 01:05:52,607
[SPEAKER_04]: And if I take that view for granted, now I will require other people to do things that way, because I am being compromised by others not following these external set of circumstances that I established upon... my safety upon.

878
01:05:53,087 --> 01:05:55,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, would you say that that is fetter of Śrīla Bhāgavatam Pāramāsa?

879
01:05:55,127 --> 01:05:55,548
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

880
01:05:55,568 --> 01:05:57,248
[SPEAKER_04]: That's exactly what Śrīla Bhāgavatam Pāramāsa is, yeah.

881
01:05:57,608 --> 01:06:02,030
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, like, it's not enough for you to just be virtuous to approve the fetter of Śrīla Bhāgavatam Pāramāsa.

882
01:06:02,230 --> 01:06:02,590
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

883
01:06:02,970 --> 01:06:05,010
[SPEAKER_04]: The view of management, as we said before

884
01:06:11,308 --> 01:06:12,130
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you mean?

885
01:06:12,170 --> 01:06:14,153
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, go to another safe place for another hunt.

886
01:06:14,173 --> 01:06:15,055
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's exactly that.

887
01:06:15,095 --> 01:06:16,317
[SPEAKER_04]: Abandoning any need for safety.

888
01:06:16,758 --> 01:06:17,820
[SPEAKER_04]: Having nothing to protect.

889
01:06:18,481 --> 01:06:20,765
[SPEAKER_04]: Relinquishing anything that would require protection.

890
01:06:21,677 --> 01:06:24,039
[SPEAKER_04]: Means then you're safe, because nothing can be threatened.

891
01:06:24,460 --> 01:06:24,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

892
01:06:25,160 --> 01:06:28,744
[SPEAKER_02]: As reflecting on sickness, that is the example.

893
01:06:28,764 --> 01:06:29,024
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

894
01:06:29,104 --> 01:06:29,624
[SPEAKER_03]: Fundamental.

895
01:06:29,805 --> 01:06:31,266
[SPEAKER_04]: Sickness, aging, death.

896
01:06:31,526 --> 01:06:31,706
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

897
01:06:31,806 --> 01:06:33,688
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the fundamental underminer.

898
01:06:33,728 --> 01:06:42,857
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing sickness as a possibility is going to cut through all the safety and comfort of having a house, having something to eat, sensual pleasures, clothing.

899
01:06:43,177 --> 01:06:45,879
[SPEAKER_03]: All of that suddenly loses perspective if

900
01:06:46,500 --> 01:06:51,405
[SPEAKER_03]: This body can basically break apart and you have to experience on your own... That's a good example.

901
01:06:51,565 --> 01:07:06,900
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how would you then sustain such... Like, say, a householder or a monk, you might get a spur of, you know, contemplating sickness that cuts through your sense of safety, but then it fades away.

902
01:07:07,200 --> 01:07:08,301
[SPEAKER_04]: Then you lose that edge.

903
01:07:08,882 --> 01:07:10,063
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you sustain that edge?

904
01:07:12,318 --> 01:07:17,064
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, this is going towards the direction of right recollection.

905
01:07:17,184 --> 01:07:20,528
[SPEAKER_03]: What is it that you're actually bringing to mind?

906
01:07:20,588 --> 01:07:23,551
[SPEAKER_04]: And the more the mind... Well, it's the same things you recollected before.

907
01:07:24,052 --> 01:07:26,555
[SPEAKER_04]: You're doing the same recollections, but there is no more edge to it.

908
01:07:27,556 --> 01:07:28,137
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that?

909
01:07:31,995 --> 01:07:44,217
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, either because the work has all been done, or, well, there can be a tendency that just thinking about something is the same as enduring the actual uncertainty, which it is not.

910
01:07:44,897 --> 01:07:46,798
[SPEAKER_04]: The present of a thought need not be experienced.

911
01:07:46,818 --> 01:07:47,718
[SPEAKER_04]: But why would that happen?

912
01:07:48,598 --> 01:07:53,239
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, something you've endured, now it just went into you, just thinking about it, and it's not felt anymore.

913
01:07:53,519 --> 01:07:59,560
[SPEAKER_03]: Because what started out as an authentic practice, gradually becomes a way of finding safety.

914
01:07:59,680 --> 01:08:00,520
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, but why?

915
01:08:03,867 --> 01:08:07,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It's removed from the situation, my present situation.

916
01:08:07,509 --> 01:08:09,070
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, or, because the mind...

917
01:08:09,090 --> 01:08:10,951
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why would you lose a sense of urgency?

918
01:08:10,991 --> 01:08:12,932
[SPEAKER_04]: Say, you're thinking the same things... Because it's unpleasant.

919
01:08:13,812 --> 01:08:15,453
[SPEAKER_04]: There was a sense of urgency.

920
01:08:16,774 --> 01:08:19,715
[SPEAKER_03]: But then it fades away, even when you really try to to get it back.

921
01:08:19,856 --> 01:08:26,099
[SPEAKER_03]: But a craving comes in to use all of that to award... to ward off unpleasant feelings.

922
01:08:26,119 --> 01:08:30,361
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it transforms into a management technique again, which is missing the point.

923
01:08:31,422 --> 01:08:31,802
[SPEAKER_04]: Could be.

924
01:08:32,775 --> 01:08:35,237
[SPEAKER_04]: You're putting it in front of you, like... Could be.

925
01:08:35,477 --> 01:08:36,998
[SPEAKER_04]: But why would you be doing all of that?

926
01:08:37,878 --> 01:08:38,479
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the real...

927
01:08:38,959 --> 01:08:45,763
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like... Say, as I said, you start contemplating sickness that comes through... cuts through the sense of safety.

928
01:08:46,384 --> 01:08:47,064
[SPEAKER_04]: And you feel that.

929
01:08:47,825 --> 01:08:49,326
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're very alert as a result of it.

930
01:08:50,927 --> 01:08:51,807
[SPEAKER_04]: But then it fades away.

931
01:08:53,268 --> 01:08:54,389
[SPEAKER_04]: Did you do something wrong?

932
01:08:54,529 --> 01:08:55,890
[SPEAKER_04]: Or it just happens on its own?

933
01:08:56,030 --> 01:08:59,012
[SPEAKER_04]: Or... How can you then get that sense of urgency back?

934
01:09:00,028 --> 01:09:00,828
[SPEAKER_04]: That the edge is gone.

935
01:09:01,149 --> 01:09:02,529
[SPEAKER_04]: Why does the edge go away?

936
01:09:02,589 --> 01:09:04,991
[SPEAKER_04]: Why can't you just sustain that edge up until you're an arahant?

937
01:09:07,132 --> 01:09:14,095
[SPEAKER_04]: Why did the Buddha have to remind even, eh... sekhas, the noble disciples, that they still have work to do?

938
01:09:14,656 --> 01:09:18,678
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would then they... why do people... why does the mind become complacent?

939
01:09:23,480 --> 01:09:25,802
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's really the question here.

940
01:09:27,262 --> 01:09:27,783
[SPEAKER_03]: Any ideas?

941
01:09:30,494 --> 01:09:31,975
[SPEAKER_03]: Why does the mind become complacent?

942
01:09:32,516 --> 01:09:37,259
[SPEAKER_04]: Even, like, you know, you're clear, so it's not on the level of, oh, I think I know what sickness is.

943
01:09:37,279 --> 01:09:38,820
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you actually know what sickness is.

944
01:09:38,981 --> 01:09:43,884
[SPEAKER_04]: It cuts through possessions and safety, but then you still become complacent.

945
01:09:46,026 --> 01:09:49,469
[SPEAKER_04]: Does that complacency... Do you ever say in it?

946
01:09:49,709 --> 01:09:51,530
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you do anything that results in it?

947
01:09:53,932 --> 01:09:54,693
[SPEAKER_04]: Or it just happens?

948
01:09:55,945 --> 01:10:00,088
[SPEAKER_00]: You come back to your safety, that you are not in that moment.

949
01:10:00,368 --> 01:10:03,611
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you can come back to it in your mind, but you don't feel that edge anymore.

950
01:10:04,571 --> 01:10:05,272
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why is that?

951
01:10:05,572 --> 01:10:06,312
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is it covered up?

952
01:10:09,935 --> 01:10:10,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing it.

953
01:10:11,996 --> 01:10:12,537
[SPEAKER_04]: That's obvious.

954
01:10:13,197 --> 01:10:14,478
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't be seeing it because it's covered up.

955
01:10:14,618 --> 01:10:15,939
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why are you not seeing it?

956
01:10:15,959 --> 01:10:16,620
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is it covered up?

957
01:10:21,103 --> 01:10:23,785
[SPEAKER_04]: We actually answered it before that.

958
01:10:25,639 --> 01:10:27,800
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no, when we said about... Well, yes, sure.

959
01:10:28,080 --> 01:10:31,920
[SPEAKER_04]: But no, when we said about the whole practice, it's about uncovering things.

960
01:10:32,921 --> 01:10:44,403
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if things get covered up, such as this edge gets covered up, means your circumstances, your choices that you make, need to be upgraded, need to change.

961
01:10:45,123 --> 01:10:49,004
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you cannot directly access that edge anymore, because it was covered up.

962
01:10:49,164 --> 01:10:49,544
[SPEAKER_04]: By what?

963
01:10:50,104 --> 01:10:53,265
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, by lack of seclusion, by company,

964
01:10:54,647 --> 01:10:59,350
[SPEAKER_04]: By distraction, by still choosing greed, aversion, delusion on some level of action.

965
01:11:01,031 --> 01:11:02,392
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you start...

966
01:11:02,712 --> 01:11:07,976
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, if you start thinking like this, you can get... then you... if the edge disappears, you use that as a symptom.

967
01:11:08,516 --> 01:11:14,160
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you don't just try to... oh, let me just contemplate and revoke that novelty of that edge of sickness.

968
01:11:14,901 --> 01:11:15,301
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no.

969
01:11:16,509 --> 01:11:17,230
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't do that.

970
01:11:17,350 --> 01:11:18,210
[SPEAKER_04]: It will be covered up.

971
01:11:18,451 --> 01:11:18,771
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

972
01:11:19,431 --> 01:11:22,273
[SPEAKER_04]: So, am I liable to covering up the sense of urgency?

973
01:11:22,313 --> 01:11:28,818
[SPEAKER_04]: There can be another line of reasoning you can use, similar to other lines of reasoning in the suttas that I would encourage.

974
01:11:29,519 --> 01:11:30,740
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's my point.

975
01:11:30,780 --> 01:11:33,162
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it doesn't get covered up on its own.

976
01:11:33,502 --> 01:11:37,205
[SPEAKER_04]: It will disappear, the edge, either because you're an arahant, exactly as you said,

977
01:11:38,106 --> 01:11:47,883
[SPEAKER_04]: All because your choices and actions and circumstances that you've been tolerating on certain levels are resulting in accumulation that covers it up.

978
01:11:48,697 --> 01:11:55,678
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even that contemplation of sickness needs to be seen in a wider context, and if this edge goes off, then, rather than...

979
01:11:55,698 --> 01:11:57,139
[SPEAKER_04]: There are only two reasons, yeah.

980
01:11:57,219 --> 01:11:58,819
[SPEAKER_04]: Either arahantship or a cover-up.

981
01:11:59,059 --> 01:11:59,259
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

982
01:11:59,699 --> 01:12:03,900
[SPEAKER_04]: Surely, it won't be on the level of a putujanas cover-up, when he just ignores and so on.

983
01:12:03,920 --> 01:12:04,180
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

984
01:12:04,540 --> 01:12:12,622
[SPEAKER_04]: But, if the edge is gone, if that uncertainty, the back onto the very start of this long talk, when you were existing as a question,

985
01:12:13,413 --> 01:12:19,234
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, the pain of that can disappear if you practice rightly, but the question remains, because the anicca remains.

986
01:12:20,574 --> 01:12:28,016
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's that anicca that then prevents you to commit, to find safety in things that are uncertain, find ownership in things that are uncertain.

987
01:12:29,376 --> 01:12:38,838
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the discomfort of that question, the sense of urgency of that question, will disappear if you're either an arahant or if you cover it up.

988
01:12:39,744 --> 01:12:43,367
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what the Buddha needed to encourage even sekkers who would become complacent.

989
01:12:44,588 --> 01:12:46,569
[SPEAKER_02]: So you can keep asking, what haven't I abandoned?

990
01:12:46,950 --> 01:12:50,292
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, is it... no, just ask yourself, do I feel the sense of urgency?

991
01:12:50,673 --> 01:12:50,833
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

992
01:12:51,413 --> 01:12:52,634
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, is it because I'm an arahant?

993
01:12:53,315 --> 01:12:56,437
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, if the answer to that is no, then it's because I'm covering it up.

994
01:12:56,958 --> 01:12:59,920
[SPEAKER_04]: And you should start feeling urgency in regard to that.

995
01:13:01,001 --> 01:13:02,722
[SPEAKER_04]: And how do I cover it up?

996
01:13:02,802 --> 01:13:04,243
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it just an accident?

997
01:13:04,784 --> 01:13:05,725
[SPEAKER_04]: Accidental cover-up?

998
01:13:05,865 --> 01:13:06,025
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

999
01:13:07,618 --> 01:13:08,599
[SPEAKER_04]: How am I spending my day?

1000
01:13:09,039 --> 01:13:10,680
[SPEAKER_04]: What are the choices I'm making throughout the day?

1001
01:13:10,920 --> 01:13:11,801
[SPEAKER_04]: What are my states of mind?

1002
01:13:11,941 --> 01:13:12,882
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how I'm covering it up.

1003
01:13:13,442 --> 01:13:16,124
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's always intentional choices.

1004
01:13:16,164 --> 01:13:20,587
[SPEAKER_04]: These mistakes, obstructions, cannot happen accidentally.

1005
01:13:20,607 --> 01:13:21,348
[SPEAKER_04]: That's my whole point.

1006
01:13:22,769 --> 01:13:27,152
[SPEAKER_04]: And, practically speaking, it's going to come down to, okay, so, am I secluded enough?

1007
01:13:28,550 --> 01:13:36,572
[SPEAKER_04]: Is most of my daily time spent in company, in work, in talking, in thinking things that are secondary?

1008
01:13:37,372 --> 01:13:47,074
[SPEAKER_04]: Or do I actually try and protect that, so that, indirectly, I protect the context of the sense of urgency being covered up?

1009
01:13:47,094 --> 01:13:48,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, those are all the obstacles for the seka.

1010
01:13:49,054 --> 01:13:49,794
[SPEAKER_03]: The lighting in work.

1011
01:13:49,814 --> 01:13:50,174
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

1012
01:13:50,374 --> 01:13:51,074
[SPEAKER_03]: The lighting in work.

1013
01:13:51,094 --> 01:13:51,735
[SPEAKER_03]: Sleeping a lot.

1014
01:13:51,775 --> 01:13:52,655
[SPEAKER_03]: Talking, sleeping.

1015
01:13:52,675 --> 01:13:53,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, talking.

1016
01:13:55,985 --> 01:14:02,269
[SPEAKER_04]: But even for a non-seeker, who wants to, as I said, start to endure things rightly, you need that sense of urgency.

1017
01:14:02,749 --> 01:14:13,195
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, you know, you can think about it when there is no distraction, when you're alone, but then you engage with duties, with speaking, with doing this, with doing that, and you kind of forget, or you don't feel the question mark anymore.

1018
01:14:13,635 --> 01:14:18,418
[SPEAKER_04]: You know it's there, theoretically, abstractly, you'll give the same answer, but you don't feel it the same way.

1019
01:14:18,678 --> 01:14:19,579
[SPEAKER_02]: That's seclusion.

1020
01:14:19,679 --> 01:14:22,060
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not putting yourself in a dangerous position.

1021
01:14:22,280 --> 01:14:22,661
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

1022
01:14:23,461 --> 01:14:24,922
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why seclusion is necessary.

1023
01:14:27,163 --> 01:14:39,592
[SPEAKER_03]: This is precisely what the Buddha says, or asked Ananda to instruct like those who have newly gone forth.

1024
01:14:40,566 --> 01:14:41,346
[SPEAKER_03]: There's these five things.

1025
01:14:42,026 --> 01:14:42,826
[SPEAKER_03]: Keep the patimokkha.

1026
01:14:43,547 --> 01:14:50,448
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, secondly, sense restraint, don't talk much, dwell bodily secluded, and work on the right view.

1027
01:14:50,908 --> 01:14:51,108
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1028
01:14:51,188 --> 01:14:55,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Those are the five things for anyone who has left the household life and goes forth.

1029
01:14:56,089 --> 01:15:08,232
[SPEAKER_03]: So, just to do that, regardless of any methods or techniques to put yourself in that situation, and then not come to morning and evening meetings, but just go and pīṇapārtha and have the patimokkha every fortnight, and other than that, you are on your own.

1030
01:15:08,452 --> 01:15:08,632
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1031
01:15:09,512 --> 01:15:14,993
[SPEAKER_03]: It's gonna... well, it seems to me... well, this is going to expose everything that we just talked about.

1032
01:15:15,834 --> 01:15:24,895
[SPEAKER_04]: So, then, the question comes down to how do you prevent... sorry, the answer comes down to the question of how do you prevent losing the edge of the sense of urgency.

1033
01:15:24,935 --> 01:15:36,538
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, start sort of, you know, reflecting upon and broadening your questioning in context, so to find all the indirect things that you are still doing,

1034
01:15:37,739 --> 01:15:45,210
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, I keep the precepts and all this, but I don't feel it anymore.

1035
01:15:45,731 --> 01:15:46,332
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, okay.

1036
01:15:46,352 --> 01:15:47,914
[SPEAKER_04]: So, are you sufficiently secluded?

1037
01:15:50,318 --> 01:15:51,539
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you sufficiently withdrawn?

1038
01:15:52,040 --> 01:15:53,162
[SPEAKER_04]: How about your mental states?

1039
01:15:54,715 --> 01:15:56,216
[SPEAKER_04]: Do they still revolve around greed?

1040
01:15:56,496 --> 01:16:02,862
[SPEAKER_04]: All these little things that are not as bad, obviously, as before, now are bad enough to cover up.

1041
01:16:03,542 --> 01:16:07,225
[SPEAKER_04]: So now you have to remove the finer ones, but now they are the impurities.

1042
01:16:07,645 --> 01:16:11,048
[SPEAKER_04]: Now they're the only impurities, thus they are the ones that are covering it up now.

1043
01:16:11,268 --> 01:16:11,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1044
01:16:12,069 --> 01:16:14,511
[SPEAKER_03]: And that can only be done once the core stuff is sort of out of the way.

1045
01:16:14,611 --> 01:16:15,372
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

1046
01:16:15,472 --> 01:16:22,437
[SPEAKER_04]: So, restraint by body, by speech, by patimokha, by precepts, sensory strain, guarding of the sense doors, sure.

1047
01:16:23,253 --> 01:16:25,514
[SPEAKER_04]: But, that was just the beginning.

1048
01:16:26,254 --> 01:16:36,018
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if the sense of urgency, if the edge, if the question mark is not felt anymore, means now there is a time for a next layer of removal of the cover-up.

1049
01:16:36,198 --> 01:16:36,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

